{"text": "The decrease in the 12-month rate between May and June 2026 reflected downward contributions from five divisions, partially offset by upward contributions from three divisions. The largest downward contribution came from transport, particularly motor fuels. There were no large, offsetting upward contributions.\n\nPrices in the transport division rose overall by 5.7% in the 12 months to June 2026, down from 6.8% the previous month (Figure 3). On a monthly basis, prices fell by 0.3% in June 2026, compared with a rise of 0.7% a year ago.\n\nThe largest downward effect behind the slowing in the transport annual rate came from motor fuels, particularly diesel.\n\nThe average price of diesel fell by 10.7 pence per litre between May and June 2026, compared with a fall of 0.6 pence per litre between May and June 2025. The average price stood at 176.4 pence per litre in June 2026.\n\nPetrol prices fell by 2.1 pence per litre in June 2026, compared with a fall of 0.5 pence per litre in June 2025. The average price stood at 155.3 pence per litre in June 2026. This is the first time petrol prices have eased since the start of the conflict in the Middle East, on 28 February 2026.\n\nThese movements resulted in overall motor fuel prices rising by 21.3% in the 12 months to June 2026, compared with a rise of 24.6% in the 12 months to May, which was the highest recorded rate since September 2022.\n\nWithin transport, there was a smaller downward effect from vehicle maintenance and repair. Prices fell by 0.2% in June 2026, compared with a rise of 2.0% in June 2025. The movements were caused by price changes for roadside recovery services.\n\nFood and non-alcoholic beverage prices rose by 1.7% in the 12 months to June 2026, down from 2.2% the previous month (Figure 4). The annual rate in June was the lowest since August 2024, when it was 1.3%. On a monthly basis, food and non-alcoholic beverage prices fell by 0.2% in June 2026, compared with a rise of 0.3% a year ago.\n\n<ts></ts>", "timeseries": [{"values": [6.4, 6.3, 6.3, 4.7, 4.2, 4.2, 4.2, 3.8, 3.8, 3.0, 2.8, 2.8, 3.1, 3.1, 2.6, 3.2, 3.5, 3.5, 3.9, 3.7, 3.4, 4.1, 4.0, 4.1, 4.2, 4.1, 4.1, 3.8, 3.5, 3.6, 3.2, 3.2, 3.4, 3.0, 3.0, 2.8], "unit": "cpih_annual_rate_pct", "freq": "1M"}, {"values": [6.8, 6.7, 6.7, 4.6, 3.9, 4.0, 4.0, 3.4, 3.2, 2.3, 2.0, 2.0, 2.2, 2.2, 1.7, 2.3, 2.6, 2.5, 3.0, 2.8, 2.6, 3.5, 3.4, 3.6, 3.8, 3.8, 3.8, 3.6, 3.2, 3.4, 3.0, 3.0, 3.3, 2.8, 2.8, 2.6], "unit": "cpi_annual_rate_pct", "freq": "1M"}, {"values": [-0.4, 0.3, 0.5, 0.0, -0.2, 0.4, -0.6, 0.6, 0.6, 0.3, 0.3, 0.1, -0.2, 0.3, 0.0, 0.6, 0.1, 0.3, -0.1, 0.4, 0.3, 1.2, 0.2, 0.3, 0.1, 0.3, 0.0, 0.4, -0.2, 0.4, -0.5, 0.4, 0.7, 0.7, 0.2, 0.1], "unit": "cpi_monthly_rate_pct", "freq": "1M"}, {"values": [130.9, 131.3, 132.0, 132.0, 131.7, 132.2, 131.5, 132.3, 133.0, 133.5, 133.9, 134.1, 133.8, 134.3, 134.2, 135.0, 135.1, 135.6, 135.4, 136.0, 136.5, 138.2, 138.4, 138.9, 139.0, 139.3, 139.3, 139.8, 139.5, 140.1, 139.5, 140.1, 141.0, 142.1, 142.4, 142.5], "unit": "cpi_index_2015_100", "freq": "1M"}, {"values": [129.0, 129.4, 130.1, 130.2, 130.0, 130.5, 130.0, 130.8, 131.6, 132.2, 132.7, 133.0, 132.9, 133.4, 133.5, 134.3, 134.6, 135.1, 135.1, 135.6, 136.1, 137.7, 138.0, 138.4, 138.5, 138.9, 138.9, 139.5, 139.4, 139.9, 139.4, 140.0, 140.8, 141.8, 142.1, 142.3], "unit": "cpih_index_2015_100", "freq": "1M"}, {"values": [374.2, 376.6, 378.4, 377.8, 377.3, 379.0, 378.0, 381.0, 383.0, 385.0, 386.4, 387.3, 387.5, 389.9, 388.6, 390.7, 390.9, 392.1, 391.7, 394.0, 395.3, 402.2, 402.9, 404.5, 406.2, 407.7, 406.1, 407.4, 405.6, 408.5, 406.4, 408.2, 411.4, 414.4, 415.3, 416.5], "unit": "rpi_all_items_index_1987_100", "freq": "1M"}, {"values": [14.8, 13.6, 12.1, 10.1, 9.2, 8.0, 6.9, 5.0, 4.0, 2.9, 1.7, 1.5, 1.5, 1.3, 1.9, 1.9, 2.0, 2.0, 3.3, 3.3, 3.0, 3.4, 4.4, 4.5, 4.9, 5.1, 4.5, 4.9, 4.2, 4.5, 3.6, 3.3, 3.7, 3.0, 2.2, 1.7], "unit": "cpi_annual_rate_food_nonalc_pct", "freq": "1M"}, {"values": [9.4, 10.5, 11.2, 11.0, 10.2, 12.9, 12.4, 1